    "body": "Employees at the Jwaneng diamond mine, which DeBeers owns jointly with the Government of Botswana.\nBy: Staff Writer November 20, 2026\nAngola plans to pay about $180 million (C$258 million) for a 10% stake in De Beers , the head of state diamond miner Endiama said, as African producers seek greater control over the industry.\nEndiama CEO José Manuel Augusto Ganga Júnior disclosed the amount on Friday, according to Reuters . The report didn’t detail how the investment would be structured.\nThe announcement comes as Botswana backs a takeover proposal from the Gareth Penny-led Global Diamond Consortium (GDC), while seeking to double its own holding in De Beers from 15% to 30%.\nThe negotiations could reshape ownership of one of the world’s best-known diamond companies, giving producing nations a larger stake in the business even as weak natural diamond demand and competition from laboratory-grown stones undermine its value.\nOwnership questions\nAnglo American (LSE: AAL) put De Beers up for sale in 2024 as part of a broader restructuring. The mining group holds 85% of the diamond company, with Botswana owning the remainder.\nBotswana President Duma Boko announced on Nov. 9 that his government had selected GDC as its preferred bidder for Anglo American’s controlling interest. Anglo said it was still working with the governments and consortium towards binding transaction documents, Reuters reported.\nIn a Nov. 12 statement, Angola’s mining ministry outlined its participation in the proposed acquisition alongside GDC, Botswana, Namibia and Oman. It did not disclose the ownership allocation among the participants.\nOn a simple proportional basis, $180 million for 10% would imply an equity value of $1.8 billion for De Beers. That calculation doesn’t establish an agreed transaction valuation because the structure of Angola’s investment hasn’t been disclosed.\nRapaport News reported on Nov. 10, citing a source, that GDC’s offer included $750 million up front and $250 million in deferred payments to Anglo, while a $500-million capital injection into De Beers remained under discussion. Those terms cannot be compared directly with Angola’s proposed investment without knowing what each amount covers.\nAfrican ambitions\nAccording to Angola’s ministry, the proposed shareholding would give the country a voice in De Beers’ strategy and help develop domestic diamond cutting, polishing and marketing.\nGanga Júnior said Angola’s proposed investment remained worthwhile despite the downturn in gem prices, Reuters reported. De Beers already explores in Angola with Endiama; the partners announced the discovery of a new kimberlite field in August 2025.\nEndiama’s CEO spoke after the inauguration of Angola’s first gold refinery in Viana, Luanda. Built and financed by Endiama, the facility has a design capacity of up to 25 kilograms of gold a day, according to the government. Print\nTAG: Africa Botswana Diamonds mining M&A\nCOMMODITY: Diamond\nREGION: Africa\nCOMPANY: Anglo American\nRepublish this article",
